# Copy this file to .env.local and fill in the values below.
#
#   cp .env.local.example .env.local
#
# The first value is the public client-owned URL. The remaining values come
# from your own Supabase project: Dashboard -> Project Settings -> API.
#
# All four are required for the complete Basecamp experience. The catalog can
# render without the service-role key, but account administration cannot.
#
# .env.local is gitignored. Never commit real values.

# The public, client-owned origin. Do not use a Vercel deployment hostname once
# a custom domain exists. Sign-in links use this value regardless of which
# deployment alias an administrator happens to open.
BASECAMP_SITE_URL=https://basecamp.example.org

# Project URL. Looks like https://abcdefghijklmnopqrst.supabase.co
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://YOUR-PROJECT-REF.supabase.co

# The ANON / publishable key — NOT the service_role key.
#
# This is deliberate and load-bearing. The entire access model of this app is
# enforced by Postgres row-level security, and the service_role key BYPASSES
# RLS. Putting a service_role key here would hand every signed-in user the whole
# catalog regardless of what they have been granted. The app has no code path
# that wants it, and there is no server-side role check to fall back on.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=YOUR-ANON-KEY


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NEEDED FOR ONBOARDING. The app runs without this: sign-in, the catalog, grants
# and the audit log are all RLS on the anon key and do not touch it. What needs
# it are the three admin routes that create, unlock and suspend ACCOUNTS —
# Add person, Issue a sign-in link, and Suspend/Restore — because those call
# Supabase Auth, which no RLS policy can reach.
#
# Without it you are back to creating each person by hand in the Supabase
# dashboard, which is the round-trip this app exists to remove.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The SERVICE_ROLE key. Note the missing NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix, and do not add one.
#
# READ THIS BEFORE FILLING IT IN. Everything above is deliberate: this app's
# access model is Postgres row-level security, and service_role bypasses RLS
# entirely. That has not changed. What this key buys is the one thing RLS cannot
# express — creating a person's identity, which lives in `auth.users`, a schema
# this app does not own.
#
# It is bounded in code, not by convention:
#
#   * It is read in exactly one function, inside src/lib/supabase/admin.ts, and
#     is never exported from it — so there is no way to obtain an unrestricted
#     client except through the check below.
#   * The privileged client is constructed only AFTER Postgres has answered
#     `basecamp.is_super_admin()` true for the caller's own token — the same
#     trust root every RLS policy consults.
#   * Callers get a facade of four VERBS with pinned arguments — not the raw
#     Supabase Auth methods — so it cannot read or write a single `basecamp`
#     table, and cannot change a password, an email or a role. Catalog rows,
#     grants, member types and the audit log stay behind RLS exactly as before.
#
# The missing NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix is the mechanism, not a style choice: Next.js
# inlines prefixed variables into the browser bundle at build time. Prefixed,
# this key would be published to every visitor.
#
# A deliberately read-only deployment may leave it unset. A complete Basecamp
# launch cannot: Add person, Issue a sign-in link and Suspend/Restore require it.
#
# Supabase Dashboard -> Project Settings -> API -> service_role
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# Optional public client ID for the built-in Authorization Code + PKCE reference
# client at /sso/reference. Register that exact app and callback in Supabase
# before setting this value. This is an identifier, never a client secret.
BASECAMP_REFERENCE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
